# Line endings — pin the repository to what it ALREADY is.
# =============================================================================================
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# `core.autocrlf = true` was in effect with no attributes file, which means git decides per
# checkout, by content heuristic, whether to write LF or CRLF into the working tree. Every git
# operation printed "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it", and that is not
# a warning about the past — it is a statement of intent about the next checkout, merge or
# `stash pop`.
#
# That intent is the hazard. Tools in tools/avatar/ write their outputs with LF, and several of
# them are byte-exact: SHA-256 pins on assets and fixtures, golden comparisons, and tests that
# assert one file reproduces another byte for byte. When git re-materialises a tracked text file
# as CRLF, those comparisons fail LOCALLY while CI — which runs on Linux and never converts —
# stays green. A failure that only one machine can see, caused by a git setting rather than by
# the code, is the most expensive kind to diagnose.
#
# WHAT THIS CHANGES TODAY: NOTHING.
# Measured before writing this file: all 2 793 tracked text blobs are already LF (`git ls-files
# --eol` reports i/lf for every one; 132 are binary, 13 are empty). Normalisation therefore
# rewrites no blob — there is nothing to convert. The only effect is on FUTURE checkouts, which
# now write LF instead of CRLF. This file describes the existing state so it stops drifting; it
# is not a migration.
#
# The .cmd shims under node_modules/.bin are deliberately left under the rule below rather than
# pinned to CRLF. They are LF today and work; forcing them to CRLF would be a behaviour change,
# not a preservation, and it is not what this file is for.

# Everything git detects as text is stored and checked out with LF. `text=auto` keeps the
# content detection, so binary files are still left alone even without an explicit rule below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The avatar pipeline is byte-exact: assets are SHA-pinned and compared as bytes. These are
# named explicitly so no future heuristic can decide one of them is text and "fix" its newlines.
*.png   binary
*.webp  binary
*.wav   binary
*.docx  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary

# Two reference files carry no extension and are binary artwork.
assets/avatar/reference/Northstar* binary
